Title: "Is Question Keyword"
Id: "5d589fff-199f-4bba-bb82-675db4cc47e4"

Text: "`is?` is the predicate form of `is`. Use `\\not` to negate it.

```mlg-fragment
x is? \set
\\not{x is? \empty.set}
```

These expressions state a type predicate or its negation without introducing a
symbol. The checker validates the subject, the referenced type, and the
requirements of the predicate; it does not prove the mathematical truth of a
negated statement. The former `is_not?` spelling is not accepted by the parser.

A type predicate in a statement position (`then:`, `that:`, `if:`, `iff:`)
uses `is?`. Binding positions such as `given:` and `suchThat:` may use bare
`is` to introduce a symbol. The same distinction applies to specification
operators: the binding form `x \"in\" X` and predicate form `x \"in\"? X`, and
the infix forms `A \:subset:/ B` and `A \:subset?:/ B`."
Id: "ddc5d29d-f8ea-4053-8d1e-496afcb301a7"
